{"id":"refine_20260929114647820","summary":"Saved the demonstrated sparse-grid line-connecting rule as a global memory.","rationale":"The solved task and its demonstration support a reusable transformation: connect successive same-color markers along rows and columns, coloring only intervening cells while preserving markers and other cells.","expectedOutcome":"On recurring instances of this pattern, the agent can recognize the rule and apply it directly; validate by checking that only gaps between consecutive markers in each row or column are filled.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_connect_successive_row_and_column_markers","title":"ARC: connect successive row and column markers","content":"For a sparse grid with background 0 and marker cells 8, draw straight lines in color 3 between successive markers sharing a row or column. Fill only the intervening cells between consecutive markers; preserve all marker cells and leave all other cells unchanged. Apply independently along rows and columns. A demonstration with marker color 4 confirmed the same pattern, with the intervening cells changed to 3.","metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Solved task-0ab32bb0 and its demonstration"},"reason":"This is a stable, reusable ARC transformation supported by a correctly solved instance and its demonstration.","after":{"id":"arc_connect_successive_row_and_column_markers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: connect successive row and column markers","content":"For a sparse grid with background 0 and marker cells 8, draw straight lines in color 3 between successive markers sharing a row or column. Fill only the intervening cells between consecutive markers; preserve all marker cells and leave all other cells unchanged. Apply independently along rows and columns. A demonstration with marker color 4 confirmed the same pattern, with the intervening cells changed to 3.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Solved task-0ab32bb0 and its demonstration"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:52.754Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:52.754Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929114716049","summary":"Broadened the existing ARC marker-connecting memory to cover the demonstrated backgrounds and marker colors.","rationale":"The demo and correct test submission support the same rule as the existing memory, while showing it applies beyond background 0 and markers 8.","expectedOutcome":"Future instances can apply the rule to different uniform backgrounds and marker colors; validate by filling only cells strictly between consecutive markers sharing a row or column and preserving all other cells.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_connect_successive_row_and_column_markers","title":"ARC: connect successive row and column markers","content":"For a grid with a uniform background and sparse marker cells, draw color 3 only in the cells strictly between consecutive markers sharing a row or column. Preserve marker cells and all other cells. A lone marker in a row or column creates no line. Demonstrated with markers 9 on background 4 and markers 0 on background 2.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Generalizes the existing rule using a successful demonstration and test while avoiding a duplicate memory.","before":{"id":"arc_connect_successive_row_and_column_markers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: connect successive row and column markers","content":"For a sparse grid with background 0 and marker cells 8, draw straight lines in color 3 between successive markers sharing a row or column. Fill only the intervening cells between consecutive markers; preserve all marker cells and leave all other cells unchanged. Apply independently along rows and columns. A demonstration with marker color 4 confirmed the same pattern, with the intervening cells changed to 3.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global","evidence":"Solved task-0ab32bb0 and its demonstration"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:52.754Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:52.754Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_connect_successive_row_and_column_markers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: connect successive row and column markers","content":"For a grid with a uniform background and sparse marker cells, draw color 3 only in the cells strictly between consecutive markers sharing a row or column. Preserve marker cells and all other cells. A lone marker in a row or column creates no line. Demonstrated with markers 9 on background 4 and markers 0 on background 2.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:52.754Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:47:22.498Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929114947366","summary":"Clarified the existing global ARC marker-connecting memory with the validated rule for preserving original cells.","rationale":"The task was solved correctly by drawing color 3 only in gaps between consecutive zero markers in each row and column, using the original grid to identify markers and spans. This confirms the need to preserve all original markers and avoid changing background outside those gaps, including at row/column span crossings.","expectedOutcome":"Future applications should identify spans from the unchanged input grid, fill only their strictly interior cells with color 3, and preserve all original markers and all cells outside those interiors.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_connect_successive_row_and_column_markers","title":"ARC: connect successive row and column markers (general, v2)","content":"For a grid with a uniform background and sparse marker cells, use the original input grid to identify markers and draw color 3 only in cells strictly between consecutive markers sharing a row or column. Preserve every original marker and every cell outside those gaps. Determine all spans from the original grid rather than newly drawn cells; where row and column spans cross, fill only eligible interior cells and never overwrite original markers. A lone marker in a row or column creates no span. This has been validated with background 1 and marker 0.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A correct submission on a fresh instance validated the rule and specifically supports using the original grid and preserving markers and other cells.","before":{"id":"arc_connect_successive_row_and_column_markers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: connect successive row and column markers","content":"For a grid with a uniform background and sparse marker cells, draw color 3 only in the cells strictly between consecutive markers sharing a row or column. Preserve marker cells and all other cells. A lone marker in a row or column creates no line. Demonstrated with markers 9 on background 4 and markers 0 on background 2.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:52.754Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:47:22.498Z","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_connect_successive_row_and_column_markers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: connect successive row and column markers (general, v2)","content":"For a grid with a uniform background and sparse marker cells, use the original input grid to identify markers and draw color 3 only in cells strictly between consecutive markers sharing a row or column. Preserve every original marker and every cell outside those gaps. Determine all spans from the original grid rather than newly drawn cells; where row and column spans cross, fill only eligible interior cells and never overwrite original markers. A lone marker in a row or column creates no span. This has been validated with background 1 and marker 0.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:46:52.754Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:49:52.302Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929115121044","summary":"Clarified the global ARC rightward-extension memory with the demonstrated per-row rule and a distinction from marker-connecting tasks.","rationale":"The demo and correct test submission show that motif rows retain their original cells while only trailing background cells to the right of the rightmost motif cell become 1; gaps to the left and other rows remain unchanged.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC tasks, extend each qualifying motif row rightward without filling internal gaps or applying the separate marker-connecting rule; validate by comparing the rightmost motif position in every row against the output.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_extend_first_patterned_row_to_the_right","title":"ARC: extend motif rows rightward from their rightmost cell","content":"In ARC grids with a uniform background and rows containing a motif run, extend each qualifying motif row to the right edge: find that row's rightmost motif cell and recolor only the background cells strictly to its right with color 1. Preserve all original motif cells, any background gaps to the left of the rightmost motif cell, and every other row/cell. Demonstrated with color-3 runs on background 8 and confirmed with color-9 rows on background 8; rows whose rightmost motif already reaches the edge need no changes. Do not confuse this with the distinct rule that connects successive markers along rows or columns.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Corrected and made the existing rightward-extension memory operationally precise using the demo and validated test result.","before":{"id":"arc_extend_first_patterned_row_to_the_right","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: extend first patterned row to the right","content":"In ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse structured motif rows, inspect the topmost motif row for a gap/continuation at its right edge. The output may mark with color 1 the non-motif cell(s) to the right of the motif on that row only, leaving other motif rows and the rest unchanged. Demonstrations: a row containing a 0/2 pattern had color 1 fill the background from the pattern’s rightmost cell to the edge; in the solved test, a row of 8s ending in a single background-5 cell had that edge cell changed to 1, while later full 8 row was unchanged. Determine the row and span from the motif; do not expand the fill vertically based only on nearby row patterns.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:50:29.765271+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:50:29.765288+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_extend_first_patterned_row_to_the_right","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: extend motif rows rightward from their rightmost cell","content":"In ARC grids with a uniform background and rows containing a motif run, extend each qualifying motif row to the right edge: find that row's rightmost motif cell and recolor only the background cells strictly to its right with color 1. Preserve all original motif cells, any background gaps to the left of the rightmost motif cell, and every other row/cell. Demonstrated with color-3 runs on background 8 and confirmed with color-9 rows on background 8; rows whose rightmost motif already reaches the edge need no changes. Do not confuse this with the distinct rule that connects successive markers along rows or columns.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:50:29.765271+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:51:28.135Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929115212959","summary":"Clarified the global ARC rightward-extension memory to require a contiguous, uninterrupted motif run before filling trailing background cells.","rationale":"The demonstrations and solved test show that only a contiguous motif run followed by background is extended with color 1; rows with gaps in the motif remain unchanged. This distinction corrected two incorrect submissions.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC tasks, extend only qualifying contiguous motif rows and preserve fragmented rows and all other cells; validate against the demonstrated row patterns.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_extend_first_patterned_row_to_the_right","title":"ARC: extend contiguous motif rows rightward","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background, inspect rows containing the non-background motif color. Extend a row only when its motif forms one contiguous, uninterrupted run and is followed immediately by background cells through the row's right edge: recolor only those trailing background cells with color 1. Leave rows with a motif fragmented by background gaps unchanged, even if their rightmost motif cell is followed by background. Do not alter fully filled rows or any other cells. Determine eligibility from the original input grid.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The demonstrations and test establish that contiguity is required; extending fragmented rows caused two wrong submissions.","before":{"id":"arc_extend_first_patterned_row_to_the_right","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: extend motif rows rightward from their rightmost cell","content":"In ARC grids with a uniform background and rows containing a motif run, extend each qualifying motif row to the right edge: find that row's rightmost motif cell and recolor only the background cells strictly to its right with color 1. Preserve all original motif cells, any background gaps to the left of the rightmost motif cell, and every other row/cell. Demonstrated with color-3 runs on background 8 and confirmed with color-9 rows on background 8; rows whose rightmost motif already reaches the edge need no changes. Do not confuse this with the distinct rule that connects successive markers along rows or columns.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:50:29.765271+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:51:28.135Z","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_extend_first_patterned_row_to_the_right","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: extend contiguous motif rows rightward","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background, inspect rows containing the non-background motif color. Extend a row only when its motif forms one contiguous, uninterrupted run and is followed immediately by background cells through the row's right edge: recolor only those trailing background cells with color 1. Leave rows with a motif fragmented by background gaps unchanged, even if their rightmost motif cell is followed by background. Do not alter fully filled rows or any other cells. Determine eligibility from the original input grid.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T11:50:29.765271+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T11:52:22.451Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929120229780","summary":"Clarified the existing global ARC separated-panel comparison memory using the additional validated example.","rationale":"The task confirms that equal corresponding cells become color 3 and unequal cells use the panels’ common/background color; the current harness already has a memory for this task family.","expectedOutcome":"Future solutions should compare panels cell by cell, preserve the panel-sized orientation, and distinguish equality markers from the default color; validate against both horizontal and vertical divider examples.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_separated_panels_mark_cellwise_equal_values","title":"ARC separated-panel cellwise match marker","content":"For ARC grids split into two equal-sized panels by a full uniform divider, compare corresponding cells in the two panels. Output a panel-sized grid, preserving the panel orientation and order. Mark positions where the values are equal with color 3; use the task’s common/background color at unequal positions. This applies to horizontal and vertical dividers. If equality markers blend with the default color in an example, use further demonstrations to disambiguate the rule. Validated on horizontal and vertical dividers, including a case where only one corresponding pair matched.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The solved instance and demonstrations reinforce the existing reusable panel-comparison rule and clarify that unequal positions use the common/background output color.","before":{"id":"arc_separated_panels_mark_cellwise_equal_values","kind":"memory","title":"ARC separated-panel cellwise match marker","content":"For ARC inputs split into two equal-sized panels by a full uniform divider, compare corresponding cells in the panels. Produce the panel-sized output. Cells with equal values in both panels become color 3; all other cells use the shared/common background color of the panels when one is apparent. Demonstrated: in the 24x7 example with 3-column panels, shared 8s became 3 and other cells remained 8. In the 9x23 instance, 11-column panels contained 4/6 on the left and 1/6 on the right; output was 6 everywhere except 3 where corresponding cells were both 6. Determine background output color from the task context rather than assuming 1. Validate panel dimensions and ensure every output row has identical length.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:00:59.492262+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:01:07.046897+00:00","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_separated_panels_mark_cellwise_equal_values","kind":"memory","title":"ARC separated-panel cellwise match marker","content":"For ARC grids split into two equal-sized panels by a full uniform divider, compare corresponding cells in the two panels. Output a panel-sized grid, preserving the panel orientation and order. Mark positions where the values are equal with color 3; use the task’s common/background color at unequal positions. This applies to horizontal and vertical dividers. If equality markers blend with the default color in an example, use further demonstrations to disambiguate the rule. Validated on horizontal and vertical dividers, including a case where only one corresponding pair matched.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:00:59.492262+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:02:37.546Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929120752328","summary":"Clarify the separated-panel shared-color fallback and its limits.","rationale":"The task-28664599 demonstrations support a panel-sized output with color 3 at corresponding locations where both panels share the same sparse foreground marker, with other cells retaining that marker color. Repeated guesses for panels with different foreground colors were wrong, so that encoding remains unproven.","expectedOutcome":"On similar tasks, apply the shared-marker rule only when the demonstrations support it; for differing foreground colors or unclear panel relevance, seek a clarifying demonstration rather than guessing. Validate by checking panel dimensions, correspondence, and output colors against the demos.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_equal_cell_matches_across_separated_panels_shared_color_fallback","title":"ARC separated-panel matches: shared-color fallback and uncertainty","content":"For ARC grids split by a uniform divider into equal-sized panels, use the demonstrations to determine the output panel and comparison rule; output is often panel-sized, but do not assume which panel is relevant when one panel is sparse or blank. In validated examples with the same sparse foreground marker in both panels, corresponding positions where both panels contain that marker are output as color 3, and other output cells use that marker color. This shared-color fallback is supported by demonstrations including task-28664599 demo pairs 2 and 6. Do not extrapolate this encoding to panels with different foreground colors: guessed defaults and overlap rules failed in task-28664599. If demonstrations do not establish the differing-color rule or panel relevance, request a demonstration before submitting.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Preserves the demonstrated shared-color behavior while explicitly limiting unsupported extrapolation that led to repeated incorrect submissions.","before":{"id":"arc_equal_cell_matches_across_separated_panels_shared_color_fallback","kind":"memory","title":"ARC equal-cell matches across separated panels; shared-color fallback","content":"For ARC grids split by a full uniform divider into equal-sized panels (horizontal or vertical), compare corresponding cells in the two panels. Build an output the size/orientation of one panel. Mark cells with color 3 where the paired input values are exactly equal. For all other cells, use the color that occurs in both panels (the common palette value); this can be a shared marker color even when the panels have different backgrounds. Demonstrated: panels with backgrounds 7 and 9 and shared marker 0 output 3 at aligned 0/0 matches and otherwise 0; panels with backgrounds 5 and 9 and shared color 9 output 3 at aligned equal cells and otherwise 9; test with backgrounds 8 and 7 and shared marker 1 had no equal aligned pairs, so output was all 1. Preserve panel alignment and crop away divider.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:04:29.814247+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:04:29.814264+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_equal_cell_matches_across_separated_panels_shared_color_fallback","kind":"memory","title":"ARC separated-panel matches: shared-color fallback and uncertainty","content":"For ARC grids split by a uniform divider into equal-sized panels, use the demonstrations to determine the output panel and comparison rule; output is often panel-sized, but do not assume which panel is relevant when one panel is sparse or blank. In validated examples with the same sparse foreground marker in both panels, corresponding positions where both panels contain that marker are output as color 3, and other output cells use that marker color. This shared-color fallback is supported by demonstrations including task-28664599 demo pairs 2 and 6. Do not extrapolate this encoding to panels with different foreground colors: guessed defaults and overlap rules failed in task-28664599. If demonstrations do not establish the differing-color rule or panel relevance, request a demonstration before submitting.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:04:29.814247+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:07:58.477Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929125856356","summary":"Added a global memory for inferring periodic ARC patterns from sparse row or diagonal markers while avoiding unsupported extrapolation.","rationale":"The demos include validated diagonal-cycle completion and repeated row patterns, while the 27×27 diagonal-marker task remained unsolved after eight demos; preserving both the demonstrated rule and the uncertainty should help without promoting a guessed solution.","expectedOutcome":"Future solutions can recognize marker-encoded periodic patterns, verify the cycle’s period, phase, orientation, and color mapping against demonstrations, and avoid guessing when evidence is insufficient.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_periodic_patterns_from_sparse_markers","title":"ARC: infer periodic patterns from sparse row or diagonal markers","content":"In ARC tasks, sparse markers may encode a periodic pattern that is expanded across the grid. Infer the pattern from demonstrations rather than assuming a fill rule. For diagonal cycles, compare cells by constant d=c−r; for row cycles, compare row indices. A demonstrated 3×3 case has input rows [4,0,0], [7,4,0], [5,7,4] and output rows [4,5,7], [7,4,5], [5,7,4]: the output repeats by diagonal class, with d=0→4, d=−1→7, and d=−2→5 (equivalently modulo 3). Other demonstrations show repeated row cycles and checker patterns. Before extrapolating, establish the period, phase, orientation, and how background cells are replaced from the examples. Do not infer a broad band, checkerboard, or period from only a few markers. A 27×27 test with diagonal 5/1 markers remained unsolved despite eight demos; its specific rule is uncertain and should not be asserted as established.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Captures demonstrated reusable periodic-pattern reasoning and the evidence-backed caution from a recurring but unresolved task family.","after":{"id":"arc_periodic_patterns_from_sparse_markers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: infer periodic patterns from sparse row or diagonal markers","content":"In ARC tasks, sparse markers may encode a periodic pattern that is expanded across the grid. Infer the pattern from demonstrations rather than assuming a fill rule. For diagonal cycles, compare cells by constant d=c−r; for row cycles, compare row indices. A demonstrated 3×3 case has input rows [4,0,0], [7,4,0], [5,7,4] and output rows [4,5,7], [7,4,5], [5,7,4]: the output repeats by diagonal class, with d=0→4, d=−1→7, and d=−2→5 (equivalently modulo 3). Other demonstrations show repeated row cycles and checker patterns. Before extrapolating, establish the period, phase, orientation, and how background cells are replaced from the examples. Do not infer a broad band, checkerboard, or period from only a few markers. A 27×27 test with diagonal 5/1 markers remained unsolved despite eight demos; its specific rule is uncertain and should not be asserted as established.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T12:59:04.295Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T12:59:04.295Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929130815639","summary":"Clarified the global ARC periodic-row-stripe memory with the demonstrated stripe-count period and row-residue alignment rule.","rationale":"The solved task showed that the cycle period is the number of observed full-width stripe occurrences, even when colors repeat, and that stripe colors align to their row indices modulo that period; earlier appearance-order guesses were incorrect.","expectedOutcome":"For similar grids, infer the cycle from stripe row positions, retain repeated-color occurrences, and fill every row by the resulting modulo pattern; validate against the stripe positions and both demonstrated cycles.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_periodic_row_stripes_infer_smallest_compatible_period","title":"ARC periodic row stripes: infer period from stripe occurrences and align by row residue","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and full-width constant-color horizontal stripes, use demonstrations to infer the vertical period. In the demonstrated task family, the period N is the number of observed non-background stripe-row occurrences; do not deduplicate occurrences just because some have the same color. Assign each observed stripe's color to its row-index residue modulo N, then fill every row across the full width using that N-row cycle. Preserve the phase implied by row indices rather than simply repeating stripe colors in their order of appearance. Examples: observed stripes at rows 2:9, 3:8, 11:2, 14:5, 20:3 imply residues 2:9, 3:8, 1:2, 4:5, 0:3 and cycle [3,2,9,8,5]; observed stripes at rows 0:5 and 5:6 imply a two-row alternating cycle [5,6]. For a test with stripes at rows 0:9, 1:7, 8:2, 27:2, 29:8, N=5 and the cycle is [9,7,2,2,8]. Check that every observed marker agrees with its residue before filling. If the demonstrations do not support this rule or imply conflicting residues, do not extrapolate blindly.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The additional solved instance validates this specific period and phase rule, and corrects prior guesses that deduplicated/reordered stripes or left background rows unchanged.","before":{"id":"arc_periodic_row_stripes_infer_smallest_compatible_period","kind":"memory","title":"ARC periodic row stripes: infer smallest compatible period","content":"For ARC grids consisting of full-width constant-color horizontal rows interspersed with background-zero rows, infer a vertical periodic sequence and fill all rows by repeating it. Treat each observed stripe at row r with color c as a phase constraint at r mod p. Find the smallest candidate period p for which all observed constraints are consistent; populate every row with the color mapped to its residue. Do not simply order observed colors by top-to-bottom appearance. Example: stripes at rows 4=3, 6=7, 17=9, 23=1 imply p=4 (residues 0->3, 2->7, 1->9, 3->1), so output rows repeat [3,9,7,1]. Earlier guesses p=5 failed. Related demonstrations show completion of periodic rows: 5x5 rows [2,0,7,0,7] output [2,7,7,2,7] (period 3); 12x12 with 9 known leading rows repeats the first 3 at bottom. Always verify the proposed period against all observed stripes before filling.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:01:45.419672+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:01:45.419685+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_periodic_row_stripes_infer_smallest_compatible_period","kind":"memory","title":"ARC periodic row stripes: infer period from stripe occurrences and align by row residue","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and full-width constant-color horizontal stripes, use demonstrations to infer the vertical period. In the demonstrated task family, the period N is the number of observed non-background stripe-row occurrences; do not deduplicate occurrences just because some have the same color. Assign each observed stripe's color to its row-index residue modulo N, then fill every row across the full width using that N-row cycle. Preserve the phase implied by row indices rather than simply repeating stripe colors in their order of appearance. Examples: observed stripes at rows 2:9, 3:8, 11:2, 14:5, 20:3 imply residues 2:9, 3:8, 1:2, 4:5, 0:3 and cycle [3,2,9,8,5]; observed stripes at rows 0:5 and 5:6 imply a two-row alternating cycle [5,6]. For a test with stripes at rows 0:9, 1:7, 8:2, 27:2, 29:8, N=5 and the cycle is [9,7,2,2,8]. Check that every observed marker agrees with its residue before filling. If the demonstrations do not support this rule or imply conflicting residues, do not extrapolate blindly.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:01:45.419672+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:08:28.382Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929131037888","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for reconstructing periodic row-color sequences when zero rows represent missing entries.","rationale":"The demonstrations show that full-width uniform rows encode a periodic color sequence: observed zero rows are replaced by the cycle, and the test was solved by selecting the smallest period consistent with every nonzero row and its phase.","expectedOutcome":"For similar grids, infer and validate the row-color cycle against all observed rows, then fill missing rows while preserving dimensions and full-row formatting; request more evidence rather than extrapolating an unsupported period.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_periodic_row_colors_with_zero_missing_entries","title":"ARC periodic row colors with zero rows as missing entries","content":"For ARC grids made of full-width uniform horizontal rows, zero-valued rows may be missing entries in a periodic sequence rather than background to preserve. Infer the smallest plausible period and phase that match every observed nonzero row color at its row index; verify all observations before filling any zeros. Then replace zero rows with the corresponding cycle color, preserving grid dimensions and full-width row format. Example: rows 1=4, 3=1, 4=4, 6=8, 8=5, and 11=5 (zero-based indices) are consistent with period 6 and cycle by residues [8,4,5,1,4,5]. Do not assume a period from a few matches if other observations contradict it; request more demonstrations when the sequence is underdetermined.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The task demonstrations and correct solution establish a reusable periodic-row completion rule distinct from simply preserving observed stripes.","after":{"id":"arc_periodic_row_colors_with_zero_missing_entries","kind":"memory","title":"ARC periodic row colors with zero rows as missing entries","content":"For ARC grids made of full-width uniform horizontal rows, zero-valued rows may be missing entries in a periodic sequence rather than background to preserve. Infer the smallest plausible period and phase that match every observed nonzero row color at its row index; verify all observations before filling any zeros. Then replace zero rows with the corresponding cycle color, preserving grid dimensions and full-width row format. Example: rows 1=4, 3=1, 4=4, 6=8, 8=5, and 11=5 (zero-based indices) are consistent with period 6 and cycle by residues [8,4,5,1,4,5]. Do not assume a period from a few matches if other observations contradict it; request more demonstrations when the sequence is underdetermined.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:10:46.220Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:10:46.220Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929131440832","summary":"Refined the global ARC rectangle-repair memory with the validated rule for restoring block interiors and removing isolated anomalies.","rationale":"The existing global memory covers isolated noise and rectangle outliers, and the new demonstration confirms that anomalous cells inside evidently regular solid blocks should be restored to the block color, while isolated anomalies outside blocks should revert to the uniform background.","expectedOutcome":"On similar ARC grids, preserve regular blocks, repair anomalous interior cells, and remove stray exterior noise; validate by checking block regularity, background consistency, and unchanged dimensions.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_remove_isolated_noise_and_repair_rectangle_outliers","title":"ARC remove isolated noise and repair rectangle outliers","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and separated solid rectangular color regions, use the demonstrated regular shapes to distinguish corruption from legitimate structure. Recolor sparse anomalous cells inside an evidently regular solid block to that block's color; replace isolated anomalous cells outside the blocks with the uniform background. Preserve legitimate blocks, all dimensions, and other unaffected cells. Do not indiscriminately delete an anomaly that lies inside a regular block. Apply this cleanup only when the block geometry and background are clear from the grid or demonstrations.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"A validated demonstration supports both restoring anomalous block-interior cells and removing isolated exterior noise.","before":{"id":"arc_remove_isolated_noise_and_repair_rectangle_outliers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC remove isolated noise and repair rectangle outliers","content":"In ARC grids with a uniform background and multiple solid rectangular color regions, occasional cells of a different color can be corruption/noise. Use demonstrations to infer cleanup: replace a non-background outlier embedded in a solid rectangle with that rectangle’s fill color; remove isolated off-color singletons by changing them to background. Preserve separate solid rectangular regions, even if their fill color differs from the dominant object color. Validate each suspicious cell by local rectangular context: in the observed task, embedded 6/7 outliers in rectangles were restored to rectangle fill, while isolated 6/7 markers became background. Do not globally remove minority colors because legitimate solid blocks can use them.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:14:17.263881+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:14:17.263902+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_remove_isolated_noise_and_repair_rectangle_outliers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC remove isolated noise and repair rectangle outliers","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and separated solid rectangular color regions, use the demonstrated regular shapes to distinguish corruption from legitimate structure. Recolor sparse anomalous cells inside an evidently regular solid block to that block's color; replace isolated anomalous cells outside the blocks with the uniform background. Preserve legitimate blocks, all dimensions, and other unaffected cells. Do not indiscriminately delete an anomaly that lies inside a regular block. Apply this cleanup only when the block geometry and background are clear from the grid or demonstrations.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:14:17.263881+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:14:48.198Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929131804089","summary":"Strengthened the global ARC rectangle-repair memory with evidence-based guidance for separating intended rectangles from noise.","rationale":"Five demonstrations show that noisy grids preserve their dimensions and background while isolated speckles are removed and corrupted cells inside supported rectangles are repaired. The failed instance repeatedly guessed rectangles from nearby pixels and incurred eight wrong submissions, so the memory should emphasize careful object-boundary validation and avoiding unsupported bounding-box completion.","expectedOutcome":"On similar tasks, the agent should repair well-supported rectangle interiors and remove noise without inventing or extending objects based on isolated pixels; validate candidate bounds against intact rows and columns, clear boundaries, and recurring object geometry.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_remove_isolated_noise_and_repair_rectangle_outliers","title":"ARC remove isolated noise and repair rectangle outliers","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background, separated solid rectangular color regions, and scattered single-cell noise or corruption, preserve the grid dimensions and background; remove unsupported noise to the background and restore corrupted cells only when they lie inside a clearly supported intended rectangle. Demonstrations show that noise can be a different color from the background or match a region's color, and that different-color cells inside a rectangle may be corruption. Infer rectangle bounds from multiple intact rows and columns, clear boundaries, and recurring object geometry or dimensions across the grid—not merely from the bounding box of nearby same-color pixels. Do not extend a clean rectangle because of a lone adjoining cell, or complete an accidental cluster of speckles into an unsupported object. Treat isolated cells and short streaks as noise unless the rectangle's geometry is independently supported. Check every candidate object's exact bounds before producing the output.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Adds validated noise-handling details from five demos and directly addresses the unsupported rectangle guesses that caused repeated failures.","before":{"id":"arc_remove_isolated_noise_and_repair_rectangle_outliers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC remove isolated noise and repair rectangle outliers","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and separated solid rectangular color regions, use the demonstrated regular shapes to distinguish corruption from legitimate structure. Recolor sparse anomalous cells inside an evidently regular solid block to that block's color; replace isolated anomalous cells outside the blocks with the uniform background. Preserve legitimate blocks, all dimensions, and other unaffected cells. Do not indiscriminately delete an anomaly that lies inside a regular block. Apply this cleanup only when the block geometry and background are clear from the grid or demonstrations.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:14:17.263881+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:14:48.198Z","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_remove_isolated_noise_and_repair_rectangle_outliers","kind":"memory","title":"ARC remove isolated noise and repair rectangle outliers","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background, separated solid rectangular color regions, and scattered single-cell noise or corruption, preserve the grid dimensions and background; remove unsupported noise to the background and restore corrupted cells only when they lie inside a clearly supported intended rectangle. Demonstrations show that noise can be a different color from the background or match a region's color, and that different-color cells inside a rectangle may be corruption. Infer rectangle bounds from multiple intact rows and columns, clear boundaries, and recurring object geometry or dimensions across the grid—not merely from the bounding box of nearby same-color pixels. Do not extend a clean rectangle because of a lone adjoining cell, or complete an accidental cluster of speckles into an unsupported object. Treat isolated cells and short streaks as noise unless the rectangle's geometry is independently supported. Check every candidate object's exact bounds before producing the output.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:14:17.263881+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:18:11.090Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132330154","summary":"Added a global memory for continuing demonstrated diagonal color rays.","rationale":"The solved task and both demonstrations show matching-color cells establishing diagonal directions, followed by continuation of those rays to the grid boundary while preserving all existing cells.","expectedOutcome":"Future ARC solutions may recognize this ray-continuation pattern, infer direction from aligned same-color cells, and extend only supported rays; validate that the extension follows the inferred diagonal and leaves other cells unchanged.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_continue_diagonal_color_rays","title":"ARC: continue established diagonal color rays","content":"In ARC grids with a uniform background, matching-color cells may establish a diagonal ray. When at least two same-color cells align diagonally, infer the direction and continue that color one cell per row along the same diagonal until the grid boundary. Preserve existing cells and unrelated colors; extend only rays supported by the input pattern. Rays may have different colors and opposite diagonal directions.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The successful task and its demonstrations show same-color diagonal alignments continued to the grid boundary, with original cells preserved.","after":{"id":"arc_continue_diagonal_color_rays","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: continue established diagonal color rays","content":"In ARC grids with a uniform background, matching-color cells may establish a diagonal ray. When at least two same-color cells align diagonally, infer the direction and continue that color one cell per row along the same diagonal until the grid boundary. Preserve existing cells and unrelated colors; extend only rays supported by the input pattern. Rays may have different colors and opposite diagonal directions.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:23:38.243Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:23:38.243Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132530843","summary":"Added a global memory capturing the uncertain diagonal-ray ARC task family and the need to infer its trigger from demonstrations.","rationale":"The trajectory shows selective ray extensions in some demonstrations, unchanged motifs in others, and repeated failed guesses when extending plausible diagonals in the test. The reusable lesson is to compare demonstrated triggers rather than assume every repeated diagonal pair extends.","expectedOutcome":"Future solutions should distinguish extension examples from unchanged controls, preserve existing cells, and avoid unsupported ray extrapolation; validate the inferred trigger against all available demonstrations before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_selective_diagonal_ray_extension_uncertain_trigger","title":"ARC selective diagonal-ray extensions: infer the trigger from demonstrations","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and small colored motifs, some task instances selectively extend colored rays along diagonals, while other motifs remain unchanged. Do not infer that every same-color diagonal pair should be extended. In the observed family, one demonstration extended a 9 ray down-right, another extended a 2 ray up-left, and another extended 8 up-left and 5 down-left; several other demonstrations were unchanged. A test containing plausible diagonal fragments of 7, 6, and 8 produced repeated incorrect outputs under guessed extension rules. For future instances, compare extended motifs against unchanged demonstrations to identify the structural trigger and direction; request demonstrations when useful, preserve existing cells, and extend only when the evidence supports the rule. The trigger remains uncertain from these examples alone.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Preserves the cross-instance lesson from a recurring ARC family while explicitly recording uncertainty and guarding against the repeated unsupported extrapolations seen in the trajectory.","after":{"id":"arc_selective_diagonal_ray_extension_uncertain_trigger","kind":"memory","title":"ARC selective diagonal-ray extensions: infer the trigger from demonstrations","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and small colored motifs, some task instances selectively extend colored rays along diagonals, while other motifs remain unchanged. Do not infer that every same-color diagonal pair should be extended. In the observed family, one demonstration extended a 9 ray down-right, another extended a 2 ray up-left, and another extended 8 up-left and 5 down-left; several other demonstrations were unchanged. A test containing plausible diagonal fragments of 7, 6, and 8 produced repeated incorrect outputs under guessed extension rules. For future instances, compare extended motifs against unchanged demonstrations to identify the structural trigger and direction; request demonstrations when useful, preserve existing cells, and extend only when the evidence supports the rule. The trigger remains uncertain from these examples alone.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:25:37.771Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:25:37.771Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132741287","summary":"Updated the global diagonal-ray memory with the demonstrated extensions, unchanged controls, and the unresolved trigger.","rationale":"The demos show diagonal rays extended in several directions, but controls—including one with diagonal pairs—remain unchanged; the task’s repeated failed guesses confirm that extending every aligned pair or leaving the grid unchanged is not a reliable rule.","expectedOutcome":"Future solutions should compare changed examples with unchanged controls, infer the ray-selection trigger from the whole motif, and avoid unsupported extrapolation; validate any proposed rule against every demo before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_selective_diagonal_ray_extension_uncertain_trigger","title":"ARC selective diagonal-ray extensions: infer the trigger from demonstrations","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored motifs, diagonal-ray extension may apply selectively. Demonstrations in this task family show several extensions: a color-5 ray continues one cell down-left; other examples extend color 2, 3, and 8 diagonally; color 1 extends up-left to the grid edge and color 7 down-right to the edge; color 5 extends up-left to the edge; and color 2 extends down-right to the edge. But unchanged controls include motifs with diagonal pairs, so do not extend every repeated diagonal pair or assume all colors/rays qualify. Infer which color and direction are selected from the whole motif and compare changed examples with unchanged controls. Preserve existing cells, stop at obstacles or grid bounds, and avoid extrapolating farther than the demonstrations support. In task-42de964b, eight attempts failed; tested guesses included extending color 5, extending color 3, extending color 6, and leaving the grid unchanged. Its motif had color 3 at (4,11),(5,10), color 6 at (5,9),(6,9), and color 5 at (6,10),(7,11), but the trigger remained unidentified. Treat that configuration as unresolved, not as evidence for any specific extension rule.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Captures the recurring extension examples, unchanged controls, and a concrete warning against repeating unsupported guesses.","before":{"id":"arc_selective_diagonal_ray_extension_uncertain_trigger","kind":"memory","title":"ARC selective diagonal-ray extensions: infer the trigger from demonstrations","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and small colored motifs, some task instances selectively extend colored rays along diagonals, while other motifs remain unchanged. Do not infer that every same-color diagonal pair should be extended. In the observed family, one demonstration extended a 9 ray down-right, another extended a 2 ray up-left, and another extended 8 up-left and 5 down-left; several other demonstrations were unchanged. A test containing plausible diagonal fragments of 7, 6, and 8 produced repeated incorrect outputs under guessed extension rules. For future instances, compare extended motifs against unchanged demonstrations to identify the structural trigger and direction; request demonstrations when useful, preserve existing cells, and extend only when the evidence supports the rule. The trigger remains uncertain from these examples alone.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:25:37.771Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:25:37.771Z","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_selective_diagonal_ray_extension_uncertain_trigger","kind":"memory","title":"ARC selective diagonal-ray extensions: infer the trigger from demonstrations","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored motifs, diagonal-ray extension may apply selectively. Demonstrations in this task family show several extensions: a color-5 ray continues one cell down-left; other examples extend color 2, 3, and 8 diagonally; color 1 extends up-left to the grid edge and color 7 down-right to the edge; color 5 extends up-left to the edge; and color 2 extends down-right to the edge. But unchanged controls include motifs with diagonal pairs, so do not extend every repeated diagonal pair or assume all colors/rays qualify. Infer which color and direction are selected from the whole motif and compare changed examples with unchanged controls. Preserve existing cells, stop at obstacles or grid bounds, and avoid extrapolating farther than the demonstrations support. In task-42de964b, eight attempts failed; tested guesses included extending color 5, extending color 3, extending color 6, and leaving the grid unchanged. Its motif had color 3 at (4,11),(5,10), color 6 at (5,9),(6,9), and color 5 at (6,10),(7,11), but the trigger remained unidentified. Treat that configuration as unresolved, not as evidence for any specific extension rule.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:25:37.771Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:27:51.463Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132822475","summary":"Clarified the global ARC diagonal-ray memory with the demonstrated continuation rule and an output-dimension check.","rationale":"Two demonstrations and the solved test show adjacent same-color diagonal pairs indicating rays that continue along the same diagonal toward the grid edge; the solved test also confirms preserving existing cells and unrelated motifs. One malformed submission was rejected, so checking output dimensions is a useful validation step.","expectedOutcome":"Future solutions should extend only evidence-supported diagonal rays, preserve other cells, and return a grid with exactly the input dimensions; validate the inferred direction and ray against the demonstrations before submitting.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_diagonal_ray_extend_aligned_pair_stop_obstacles","title":"ARC extend diagonal rays from aligned color pairs","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and same-color cells forming an adjacent diagonal pair, demonstrations may indicate a ray continuing in that diagonal direction. Extend the color along the demonstrated continuation toward the grid edge, preserving the pair and all unrelated cells. Infer the continuation direction from the pair's placement and demonstrations; do not assume both directions should be extended or extrapolate a ray without support. Stop at the grid boundary or at obstacles when the task examples establish that behavior. Before submitting, verify that the output has exactly the input height and width.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The current demos and successful test validate diagonal continuation from adjacent same-color pairs, while the rejected attempt shows the importance of verifying output dimensions.","before":{"id":"arc_diagonal_ray_extend_aligned_pair_stop_obstacles","kind":"memory","title":"ARC extend diagonal rays from aligned color pairs","content":"For ARC grids with uniform background and colored cells forming adjacent diagonal pairs, the pair indicates a straight diagonal ray. Extend the diagonal from its endpoints through empty background cells, preserving existing cells and stopping at the grid edge or an occupied cell. Demonstration: color-3 pair on down-right diagonal was extended backward up-left to edge; color-2 pair on down-right diagonal extended up-left, stopping forward where another color occupied the next cell. In test, color 2 at (r6,c3),(r7,c4) was continued up-left with 2 at r5c2,r4c1,r3c0; forward continuation stopped before occupied r8c5. Preserve all original cells and background.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{},"source":"agent","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:26:03.014519+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:26:03.014537+00:00","version":1},"after":{"id":"arc_diagonal_ray_extend_aligned_pair_stop_obstacles","kind":"memory","title":"ARC extend diagonal rays from aligned color pairs","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and same-color cells forming an adjacent diagonal pair, demonstrations may indicate a ray continuing in that diagonal direction. Extend the color along the demonstrated continuation toward the grid edge, preserving the pair and all unrelated cells. Infer the continuation direction from the pair's placement and demonstrations; do not assume both directions should be extended or extrapolate a ray without support. Stop at the grid boundary or at obstacles when the task examples establish that behavior. Before submitting, verify that the output has exactly the input height and width.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:26:03.014519+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:28:32.092Z","version":2},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929132942987","summary":"Added a global memory for evidence-based diagonal ray extension, including the demonstrated directions and cautions against unsupported extensions.","rationale":"The solved task showed that distinct same-color diagonal alignments can extend in different directions, and two incorrect submissions demonstrate the risk of omitting a supported ray or adding an unsupported reverse ray.","expectedOutcome":"Future solutions can use demo pairs to infer which aligned motifs extend and in which direction, preserve existing cells, and avoid overwriting other colors; validate each proposed ray against all demonstrations and controls.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_demonstrated_diagonal_ray_directions_and_controls","title":"ARC diagonal rays: infer each extension direction from demonstrations","content":"For ARC grids with sparse same-color diagonal alignments, use demonstrations to determine which alignments trigger extensions and the direction for each ray. Extend only through background in the supported outward direction, preserving all original cells and stopping before any occupied cell of another color. A demonstrated case extended 7 down-left from a pair at (r3,c4) and (r4,c3), 6 down-right from (r3,c5) and (r4,c6), and 9 up-left from (r1,c3) and (r2,c4), adding 9 at (r0,c2). Do not assume every diagonal pair extends, or extend both directions by default: compare changed examples with unchanged controls and validate each proposed ray against all demonstrations. Keep input dimensions unchanged.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The solved task provides specific evidence about per-ray direction and controls, while the two wrong attempts show that omitting a supported extension and adding reverse extensions are both failure modes.","after":{"id":"arc_demonstrated_diagonal_ray_directions_and_controls","kind":"memory","title":"ARC diagonal rays: infer each extension direction from demonstrations","content":"For ARC grids with sparse same-color diagonal alignments, use demonstrations to determine which alignments trigger extensions and the direction for each ray. Extend only through background in the supported outward direction, preserving all original cells and stopping before any occupied cell of another color. A demonstrated case extended 7 down-left from a pair at (r3,c4) and (r4,c3), 6 down-right from (r3,c5) and (r4,c6), and 9 up-left from (r1,c3) and (r2,c4), adding 9 at (r0,c2). Do not assume every diagonal pair extends, or extend both directions by default: compare changed examples with unchanged controls and validate each proposed ray against all demonstrations. Keep input dimensions unchanged.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:29:53.015Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:29:53.015Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929133202284","summary":"Updated the global diagonal-ray memory to emphasize demo-by-demo rule validation and avoid unsupported extrapolation.","rationale":"Demonstrations showed several extensions and an unchanged control, but the test combined colors 1 and 4 and eight candidate outputs all failed; a same-color diagonal pair alone does not determine the active ray or direction.","expectedOutcome":"Future solutions should compare candidate rules against every extension and control, identify the triggering motif and ray endpoints, and request more demonstrations when the rule remains underdetermined; validate by reproducing all known examples before submission.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_selective_diagonal_ray_extension_uncertain_trigger","title":"ARC selective diagonal-ray extensions: require demo-validated triggers","content":"For uniform-background ARC grids with sparse diagonal motifs, do not infer a ray extension from a same-color diagonal pair alone. Use all demonstrations—including unchanged controls—to determine which motif and color trigger an extension, its direction, and whether one or both ends continue. Demonstrated cases in this family include a diagonal 7 pair extended up-right, a diagonal 0 pair extended down-left, and 4 rays extended along their demonstrated diagonals; an orthogonal 4-pair control remained unchanged. These examples do not establish a universal color- or pair-based rule. Track existing colors, branches, obstacles, and both ray endpoints; preserve original cells and dimensions. Before submitting, check that the proposed transformation reproduces every extension and leaves every control unchanged. If multiple hypotheses remain consistent with the demonstrations, request another demonstration rather than guessing. In one test with 1 and 4 motifs, eight speculative outputs—including various 1/4 extensions and unchanged input—were all incorrect, underscoring that the trigger was not resolved by the available examples.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The trajectory provides repeated evidence that guessing ray direction or active color from isolated motif features fails; the memory should prioritize whole-demo validation and explicit uncertainty.","before":{"id":"arc_selective_diagonal_ray_extension_uncertain_trigger","kind":"memory","title":"ARC selective diagonal-ray extensions: infer the trigger from demonstrations","content":"For ARC grids with a uniform background and sparse colored motifs, diagonal-ray extension may apply selectively. Demonstrations in this task family show several extensions: a color-5 ray continues one cell down-left; other examples extend color 2, 3, and 8 diagonally; color 1 extends up-left to the grid edge and color 7 down-right to the edge; color 5 extends up-left to the edge; and color 2 extends down-right to the edge. But unchanged controls include motifs with diagonal pairs, so do not extend every repeated diagonal pair or assume all colors/rays qualify. Infer which color and direction are selected from the whole motif and compare changed examples with unchanged controls. Preserve existing cells, stop at obstacles or grid bounds, and avoid extrapolating farther than the demonstrations support. In task-42de964b, eight attempts failed; tested guesses included extending color 5, extending color 3, extending color 6, and leaving the grid unchanged. Its motif had color 3 at (4,11),(5,10), color 6 at (5,9),(6,9), and color 5 at (6,10),(7,11), but the trigger remained unidentified. Treat that configuration as unresolved, not as evidence for any specific extension rule.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:25:37.771Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:27:51.463Z","version":2},"after":{"id":"arc_selective_diagonal_ray_extension_uncertain_trigger","kind":"memory","title":"ARC selective diagonal-ray extensions: require demo-validated triggers","content":"For uniform-background ARC grids with sparse diagonal motifs, do not infer a ray extension from a same-color diagonal pair alone. Use all demonstrations—including unchanged controls—to determine which motif and color trigger an extension, its direction, and whether one or both ends continue. Demonstrated cases in this family include a diagonal 7 pair extended up-right, a diagonal 0 pair extended down-left, and 4 rays extended along their demonstrated diagonals; an orthogonal 4-pair control remained unchanged. These examples do not establish a universal color- or pair-based rule. Track existing colors, branches, obstacles, and both ray endpoints; preserve original cells and dimensions. Before submitting, check that the proposed transformation reproduces every extension and leaves every control unchanged. If multiple hypotheses remain consistent with the demonstrations, request another demonstration rather than guessing. In one test with 1 and 4 motifs, eight speculative outputs—including various 1/4 extensions and unchanged input—were all incorrect, underscoring that the trigger was not resolved by the available examples.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:25:37.771Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:32:10.379Z","version":3},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929133303487","summary":"Added a global memory for the demonstrated ARC rule that places the input and its three rotations in a 2×2 output.","rationale":"Two demonstrations show the same quadrant arrangement: original top-left, clockwise rotation top-right, counterclockwise rotation bottom-left, and 180-degree rotation bottom-right. The solved test also reinforces the need to compute each rotation exactly.","expectedOutcome":"On similar tasks, construct a grid with doubled height and width by rotating the input into each specified quadrant; validate each quadrant against rotation formulas and preserve all cells.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_input_and_rotations_in_four_quadrants","title":"ARC: arrange the input and its rotations in four quadrants","content":"For ARC tasks demonstrated by a square input grid and an output twice its height and width, the output may be a 2×2 arrangement of the input and its rotations: top-left is unchanged; top-right is rotated 90° clockwise; bottom-left is rotated 90° counterclockwise; bottom-right is rotated 180°. For an n×n input A, compute rotations exactly: clockwise C[i][j]=A[n-1-j][i], counterclockwise L[i][j]=A[j][n-1-i], and half-turn H[i][j]=A[n-1-i][n-1-j]. Concatenate the corresponding quadrants without changing colors or dimensions. Check the quadrant placement and formulas rather than transposing or rotating manually. This rule is supported by demonstrations on 2×2 and 12×12 inputs.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The two demonstrations and solved test support a precise, reusable transformation rule.","after":{"id":"arc_input_and_rotations_in_four_quadrants","kind":"memory","title":"ARC: arrange the input and its rotations in four quadrants","content":"For ARC tasks demonstrated by a square input grid and an output twice its height and width, the output may be a 2×2 arrangement of the input and its rotations: top-left is unchanged; top-right is rotated 90° clockwise; bottom-left is rotated 90° counterclockwise; bottom-right is rotated 180°. For an n×n input A, compute rotations exactly: clockwise C[i][j]=A[n-1-j][i], counterclockwise L[i][j]=A[j][n-1-i], and half-turn H[i][j]=A[n-1-i][n-1-j]. Concatenate the corresponding quadrants without changing colors or dimensions. Check the quadrant placement and formulas rather than transposing or rotating manually. This rule is supported by demonstrations on 2×2 and 12×12 inputs.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:33:12.670Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:33:12.670Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929133445969","summary":"Added a global ARC memory for doubled-grid demonstrations whose top-left quadrant copies the input while the remaining quadrants show no supported transformation.","rationale":"Across eight demonstrations, output dimensions were doubled and the input was preserved in the top-left quadrant, but the other quadrants did not support tiling, reflection, or scaling; several guesses based on those patterns failed.","expectedOutcome":"On similar tasks, the agent should preserve the demonstrated top-left quadrant, test candidate rules against every demo, and avoid presenting unsupported fills of the remaining quadrants as reliable.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"create","kind":"memory","id":"arc_doubled_grid_irregular_other_quadrants","title":"ARC doubled grids with irregular non-input quadrants","content":"In one demonstrated ARC task family, the output has exactly twice the input height and width, with the original input copied unchanged into the top-left quadrant. Across eight demos with input sizes 1×1, 7×7, 8×8, 10×10, 11×11, and 13×13, the other three quadrants contained dense, irregular values drawn from the input's palette. They did not follow a consistent tiling, reflection, or nearest-neighbor scaling rule. For similar tasks, verify the doubled dimensions and top-left copy, then compare candidate rules for the other quadrants against all demos. Do not assume repetition, symmetry, background fill, or arbitrary palette sampling without evidence; if the remaining cells have no inferable rule, recognize that the demos do not determine a reliable output rather than confidently guessing.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"Records a repeated structural observation and guards against several unsupported extrapolations that produced incorrect submissions.","after":{"id":"arc_doubled_grid_irregular_other_quadrants","kind":"memory","title":"ARC doubled grids with irregular non-input quadrants","content":"In one demonstrated ARC task family, the output has exactly twice the input height and width, with the original input copied unchanged into the top-left quadrant. Across eight demos with input sizes 1×1, 7×7, 8×8, 10×10, 11×11, and 13×13, the other three quadrants contained dense, irregular values drawn from the input's palette. They did not follow a consistent tiling, reflection, or nearest-neighbor scaling rule. For similar tasks, verify the doubled dimensions and top-left copy, then compare candidate rules for the other quadrants against all demos. Do not assume repetition, symmetry, background fill, or arbitrary palette sampling without evidence; if the remaining cells have no inferable rule, recognize that the demos do not determine a reliable output rather than confidently guessing.","path":"general","scope":"global","reference":{},"arguments":{},"metadata":{"scope":"global"},"source":"refine","created_at":"2026-09-29T13:34:55.703Z","updated_at":"2026-09-29T13:34:55.703Z","version":1},"applied":true}],"harnessStatePath":"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-primeagentlearner-1790682188/attempts/097eda31949a4b61b5f7193c1d3e6907/system/state/agent/harness/harness_state.json","scope":"global"}
{"id":"refine_20260929133923304","summary":"Strengthened the existing global memory for ARC outputs that arrange an input and its three rotations in 2×2 blocks.","rationale":"The solved 3×3 demo directly verifies the quadrant order: original top-left, 90° clockwise top-right, 90° counterclockwise bottom-left, and 180° bottom-right. Other doubled-grid demos show that doubling dimensions and copying the top-left alone do not establish this rotation rule.","expectedOutcome":"On matching tasks, construct each full-input quadrant using the demonstrated orientation and verify dimensions and quadrant contents against the demos, without inferring rotations from doubled dimensions alone.","appliedEdits":[{"action":"update","kind":"memory","id":"arc_input_and_three_rotations_in_2x2_blocks","title":"ARC: arrange the input and its three rotations in 2×2 blocks","content":"For ARC tasks whose demonstrations show an output twice the input height and width, check whether it is a 2×2 block arrangement of the full input and its rotations. In the demonstrated 3×3 case, the top-left quadrant is the input unchanged, the top-right is rotated 90° clockwise, the bottom-left is rotated 90° counterclockwise, and the bottom-right is rotated 180°. Preserve each quadrant's full dimensions and verify every orientation against the demos before applying the rule. Do not infer this arrangement from doubled dimensions or a copied top-left quadrant alone: other doubled-grid task families may have unsupported or irregular contents in the remaining quadrants.","metadata":{"scope":"global"},"reason":"The solved demo confirms the precise quadrant order and distinguishes this validated rotation rule from other doubled-grid examples.","before":{"id":"arc_input_and_three_rotations_in_2x2_blocks","kind":"memory","title":"ARC input and three rotations in 2x2 blocks","content":"For ARC tasks where a 2×2 input [a b; c d] maps to a 4×4 output, check whether output is the four rotational orientations arranged as